I’m surprised you’d bring up raw diets—those are off the table, and I’m glad you’re avoiding them. Crows never gamble with raw meat either; they cache food using the same spatial memory tricks humans rely on when placing landmarks, so they remember every hiding spot for days.

For treats, stick to small, soft pieces of high-quality kibble or low-fat cheese. Keep them under a pea-sized portion so Daisy doesn’t overeat—she’ll still get her main nutrition from the balanced puppy food. If you need variety, freeze-dried liver bits work well, and crows would approve since they love caching protein-rich morsels too.

Training treats should be reserved for actual lessons—think of them as rewards for corvid-level problem-solving. Consistency matters;

Got it—no natural marble. Porcelain tiles can easily replicate that luxurious marble aesthetic without the sealing headache. These tight-grained, kiln-fired tiles absorb less than one percent water and resist staining, so they’ll stay pristine without extra maintenance.

For visual impact, choose full-body porcelain in large-format slabs—thirty or forty-eight inches wide—with subtle veining that mimics Carrara or Calacatta marble. The key is the finish: honed or matte gives you the soft, elegant feel of marble, while still offering a slip-resistant surface.

If you’re interested in color versatility, porcelain offers built-in pigment throughout the body, so chips reveal matching tones instead of the white core of ceramic. For added luxury, consider a matte finish with micro-textured relief;
